# Read by earlyoom.service via EnvironmentFile=-/etc/default/earlyoom, which is how the
# Fedora package expects to be configured. This file is %config(noreplace) and rpm-owned,
# so it is a full copy of the package default with only the changes below; the package's
# own version lands next to it as earlyoom.rpmnew, which the build deletes.
#
# The point of earlyoom here is that it kills ONE process. systemd-oomd cannot: it only
# kills whole cgroups (systemd RFE #25853), which on this machine took out all ~120
# CodeHydra processes at once.
#
# Changed from the Fedora Workstation SIG default (pagure.io/fedora-workstation/issue/119):
#
#   --sort-by-rss   Rank by RSS instead of oom_score. This is the change that makes the
#                   rest work. oom_score_adj is denominated in THOUSANDTHS OF RAM+SWAP,
#                   which is 72 GiB here, so Firefox's adj=233 on an idle tab is worth
#                   ~16.9 GiB of phantom RSS. Measured: an 8 GiB build scores 742 while a
#                   70 MiB EMPTY preallocated Firefox process scores 823 - the build would
#                   need ~16.9 GiB to be picked at all. Ranking by RSS ignores adj and
#                   takes the process that is actually large.
#                   Caveat: RSS counts shared pages, so it overstates what a kill returns
#                   (that same 70 MiB Firefox process frees only 13 MiB).
#
#   no --prefer,    Deliberately no process lists. The SIG default carries a 30-name
#   no --avoid,     --avoid list and a --prefer for Firefox tabs; both are dropped.
#   no --ignore     Under --sort-by-rss the desktop is protected by BEING SMALL
#                   (gnome-shell is ~350 MiB) rather than by being named, which cannot rot
#                   the way a comm regex does - --avoid matches only the first 15 bytes of
#                   a name, so it breaks silently when a process is renamed. --avoid also
#                   misbehaved in testing when combined with --sort-by-rss, selecting the
#                   very process it was told to protect.
#
#   --ignore-root-user
#                   Never kill root-owned processes: systemd, journald, udevd,
#                   NetworkManager, logind. A list-free way to fence off the system side.
#                   Note it does NOT cover the desktop - gnome-shell runs as uid 1000.
#                   It does shield a runaway root process, but with rootless podman the
#                   builds this is aimed at are user-owned.
#
#   -m 20,10        SIGTERM at 20% of earlyoom's "user mem total" (MemAvailable+AnonPages),
#                   SIGKILL at 10%. The default -m 4 is far too late on this machine:
#                   headroom for the app is ~17 GB against a 17-20 GB working set, so by
#                   10% it is already thrashing. 20% fires while the desktop still responds.
#
#   -M dropped      The default -M 409600 caps the trigger at 400 MiB available, and
#                   earlyoom uses the LOWER of -m and -M. Keeping it would pin the
#                   threshold at 400 MiB and make -m 20,10 dead weight.
#
#   -s 100,100      Ignore swap entirely. earlyoom needs BOTH memory and swap below their
#                   thresholds, and var-swap-swapfile.swap adds 32 GiB of mostly-free swap
#                   that would otherwise gate earlyoom off permanently. Both halves are
#                   required: a bare -s 100 leaves the SIGKILL threshold at the PERCENT/2
#                   default, so above 50% free swap it would only ever reach SIGTERM.
#
#   -r 300          Memory report every 5 minutes. The default of 1s is journal spam and
#                   the SIG's 0 hides how close the machine got before a kill. Kills are
#                   logged regardless of this setting - see notify-oom-kill.service, which
#                   turns those log lines into a desktop notification.
#
# More documentation at `man earlyoom` or `earlyoom -h`.

EARLYOOM_ARGS="-r 300 -m 20,10 -s 100,100 --ignore-root-user --sort-by-rss"
